::ALG2_U02_L04_GP01::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L04_GP01
MLA Standard: Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What are the zeros of f(x) = (x - 5)(x + 1)?
{
=A. x = 5 and x = -1 #Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.
~B. x = -5 and x = 1 #The signs are reversed.
~C. x = 0 and x = 5 #x = 0 is not a zero here.
~D. x = 5 and x = 1 #Only x = 5 is correct.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_GP02::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L04_GP02
MLA Standard: Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the multiplicity of x = 3 in f(x) = (x - 3)²(x + 4)?
{
~A. 1 #The exponent on the factor is not 1.
~B. 3 #This is the zero value, not the multiplicity.
=C. 2 #Correct. The factor x - 3 is repeated twice.
~D. 4 #The other factor does not change this multiplicity.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_GP03::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L04_GP03
MLA Standard: Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
At a zero with even multiplicity, what does the graph usually do?
{
~A. crosses the x-axis #That is typical of odd multiplicity.
=B. touches and turns around #Correct. Even multiplicity usually touches and turns.
~C. has no x-intercept #It is still an x-intercept.
~D. becomes the y-intercept #The zero remains an x-intercept.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_GP04::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L04_GP04
MLA Standard: Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
At a zero with odd multiplicity, what does the graph usually do?
{
~A. touches only #This is typical of even multiplicity.
~B. turns before the axis #Odd multiplicity crosses the axis.
~C. stays above the axis #This is not guaranteed.
=D. crosses the x-axis #Correct. Odd multiplicity usually crosses.
}

::ALG2_U02_L04_GP05::
Question ID: ALG2_U02_L04_GP05
MLA Standard: Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which zero comes from the factor x + 6?
{
~A. x = 6 #The sign is reversed.
=B. x = -6 #Correct. x + 6 = 0 gives x = -6.
~C. x = 0 #This does not make x + 6 equal 0.
~D. x = 1 #This does not make x + 6 equal 0.
}

